## GitHub implementation (`.github/workflows/`)

Two workflows mirror the same concept:

- **`ci.yml`** runs on pull requests and pushes to `main`: a `quality` job
  (`lint`/`deps-check`/`audit`/`test`) and a `test-matrix` job (`poe test-all`). Every checkout
  uses `fetch-depth: 0` + `fetch-tags` so hatch-vcs and commitizen can see the tag history.
- **`release.yml`** runs on pushes to `main`:
    - the **`bump`** job runs `cz bump`, pushes the commit + tag, and exposes `bumped` and
      `version` as job outputs;
    - **`publish`**, **`docs`**, and **`github-release`** each `needs: bump` and run only
      `if: needs.bump.outputs.bumped == 'true'`. `publish` does `uv build` then `uv publish`,
      using PyPI **OIDC Trusted Publishing** by default (tokenless, via `id-token: write`) or a
      private index when the repo variable `PUBLISH_TARGET=private-index` is set. `docs` deploys
      versioned docs to the `gh-pages` branch with mike; `github-release` creates the release.

GitHub-pushed commits/tags don't re-trigger workflows, which (together with `[skip ci]`)
prevents loops without a PAT. Required settings (workflow write permissions, branch-protection
bypass, Pages source, PyPI trusted publisher) are under "Repository settings → GitHub" in
[Getting Started](getting-started.md).
